  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms return and increase along with \nincreasingly more humid for the first half of next week. There is \nalso the potential for areas of heavy rain and some strong to severe\nthunderstorms Tuesday.\n\nAs a strong upper-level ridge expands into the Plains early next \nweek, northwesterly flow aloft will occur across much of the East. \nThis ridge gets pushed back some as a potentially strong upper-level \ntrough in eastern Canada sends spokes of energy southeastward across \nthe Great Lakes and Northeast. This will initially allow for the \nupper-level trough to become more carved out across the Northeast by \nlater this weekend. Some guidance maintains a vigorous shortwave \nriding over the ridge and sliding southeastward across the Great \nLakes Monday and then across the Northeast during Tuesday. This \nresults in some model solutions continuing to close off the trough \nacross the Northeast Tuesday into Wednesday. Some other guidance is \nslower and weaker and thus does not close it off or briefly closes \nit off a day or two later. The upper-level system will drive surface \nlow pressure and a cold front our way Tuesday into Wednesday. It \nwill become much more humid during the first half of next week, with \ndew points looking to surge on Tuesday.\n\nThis will all result in an increase in convective chances Monday and \nespecially Tuesday into Wednesday. While there remains some \ndifferences among the model guidance on the timing and strength of \nthe features diving southeastward early next week, the setup would \ncontinue to favor upstream convective development given \nnorthwesterly flow aloft. This would be enhanced if the trough aloft \ncloses off. Deeper moisture (dew points into the low 70s) advecting \nnorthward ahead of this feature with eventual cooling aloft and \nstronger forcing for ascent should result in widespread showers and \nsome thunderstorms which maximizes on Tuesday. Depending on the \nevolution of this system, some areas of heavy rainfall (precipitable \nwater values increase to 1.5-2.00 inches) and some severe \nthunderstorms are possible. The intensity and coverage of \nthunderstorms though may partially depend on the prior days \nconvective development and then its evolution downstream.",
